{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:d2adc0a1fad83dc359b9b10550d05c3b551685dbd85ca6f9f68721a7ecdc4785", "sample_idx": 31, "model_name": "abacusai/dracarys-llama-3.1-70b-instruct", "content": "<<The Media Minefield>>\n\n\nHand in hand with the rise of social media, there has been a growing concern about the impact of these platforms on our mental health. As we navigate the complex course of online interactions, it's easy to feel like we're lost in a minefield of conflicting information and opinions. A stranger's comment can be enough to send our self-esteem plummeting, making it harder to find our way through the noise.\n\n\nIn recent years, there has been a surge in media coverage about the negative effects of social media on our mental wellbeing. Studies have shown that excessive social media use can lead to increased stress, anxiety, and depression. As we strive to present a perfect online persona, we often forget to prioritize our real-life relationships and experiences. It's time to take a step back and re-evaluate our relationship with social media, and find a healthier balance that allows us to bask in the warmth of the sun without getting burned.\n\n\nAs we move forward, it's essential to recognize the importance of media literacy in navigating the complex online landscape. By being more mindful of the information we consume and the platforms we use, we can begin to reclaim our online spaces and create a more positive and supportive community. It's time to take back control of our online lives and create a course that prioritizes our mental health and wellbeing, rather than letting social media dictate our every move.", "logprobs": [{"token": "<<", "logprob": -0.6818355917930603}, {"token": "The", "logprob": -2.3986618518829346}, {"token": " Media", "logprob": -3.6065833568573}, {"token": " Mine", "logprob": -0.5999580025672913}, {"token": "field", "logprob": -0.006020273081958294}, {"token": ">>", "logprob": -1.1399022340774536}, {"toke